{"kind":"request","text":"Build the user documentation set, anchored so each page flares when the behaviour it describes changes.","body":{"conditions":"The audience is technical, fluent in everyday git but not in its internals; plain language per AGENTS.md. The set covers why (the problem gitseq solves and the shape of its answer), what (components, the record, actors and authority, the work loop, and what staleness does and does not cover), how (one end-to-end path plus recipes for running a work loop, publishing and auditing from a fresh clone, deploying a resident, and configuring an agent), and reference for every gs subcommand and every MCP tool.\n\nAnchoring is the point, not a garnish. Each page carries front matter naming the durable acts that govern the behaviour it describes, and each page ships with its own artifact statement resting on those acts, so a flare names a page a reader can act on rather than the set as a whole. Page boundaries follow what would falsify a page, which is why the gs reference is one page per subcommand. This request rests on the world-describing-artifacts request and is sequenced after it: prose written before that convention lands has to be re-anchored, so the convention is a prerequisite and not a parallel track.\n\nSatisfaction is decided by four mechanical gates, each committed to the repository so the docs build under the project discipline rather than by inspection. First, surface completeness: a test diffs the CLI subcommand and flag tables against the reference pages, and the MCP tools list against the tool pages, and fails on either gap. Second, examples run: every command block in the docs executes against a scratch workroom. Third, no empty basis: every page has a non-empty rests_on set, checked through the unbridged-artifact mark that the world-describing-artifacts request introduces. Fourth, the flare test: superseding one governing act makes exactly the dependent pages stale and no others. The acceptance instance to use is the open request for an honest gs verify equivocation posture — when that merges, the gs verify page flares and nothing else does.\n\nState one known limit rather than hide it: staleness does not currently propagate through ineffective bases, which is carried by the lying-by-omission request, so a page anchored to an act that lands ineffective goes quiet rather than stale.\n\nDeliver as child requests resting on this one, each a single branch reviewed at one exact head: skeleton, front matter convention and the four gates, with no prose migration; gs subcommand reference; MCP tools, event identifiers, limits and glossary; concepts; how-to including a reworked getting-started; and close-out, retiring the mixed reference page and linking the set from README. Obtain independent exact-head review before each merge.\n\nNon-goals: SKILL.md, which is the normative agent contract and not user documentation; notes/, which is dated and unmaintained; and the design note.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}